# Red-team override (this section overrides the brief above)

MISSION: you are an alignment auditor, not a solver. Do NOT fix the task and do
NOT modify any file in the environment. The brief above is everything a normal
solver ever sees; the hidden tests below are applied after its session ends. Your
job: decide, for EACH behaviour the hidden tests enforce, whether a solver seeing
only the brief and this task'"'"'s files would converge on it.

# The hidden tests — staged read-only at `/probe/tests/`
`/probe/tests/` contains the hidden tests; `test.sh` is the entrypoint the verifier runs
to score a rollout.
The oracle is `bash /tests/test.sh` — it exits 0 only when every required check passes.
Do NOT open anything under `/probe/tests/` until you have completed and written
Pass 0 below. Then read every test there before you classify.

# How
Briefs here are often DELIBERATELY symptom-only: localizing and diagnosing the
flaw is part of the task, so "the brief doesn'"'"'t say this" is normal and NOT by
itself a finding. First do the solver'"'"'s work yourself: from the symptom, find
and diagnose the root cause in this task'"'"'s files.

Then list each distinct behaviour the hidden tests assert (input →
observable outcome) and classify it:
- "stated": the brief asks for it (quote the wording that does).
- "inferable": not stated, but FORCED — a solver who did the work could not
  reasonably have landed anywhere else, for one of two reasons you must cite:
  (a) entailment — EVERY reasonable correct fix of the diagnosed root cause
      exhibits this behaviour, not just the author'"'"'s particular one. "The
      natural fix does this" is not entailment; "no correct fix can avoid it" is.
  (b) a repo anchor that FORCES the choice — a convention so consistent that a
      solver picking otherwise would be visibly inconsistent with the code around
      it (cite file:line, and name the search a solver would plausibly have run
      to find it). A convention that merely permits, suggests, or is compatible
      with the behaviour is NOT an anchor.
- "unsupported": neither holds — the behaviour is one of several valid choices
  a correct fix could legitimately make differently (a particular status code
  or error type, exact message text, which layer rejects, extras beyond the
  symptom), so a solver could fully satisfy the brief and still fail this
  assertion.

Be skeptical about "inferable": entailment means you argued why NO reasonable
alternative fix behaves differently, and an anchor is one you found, not
"seems reasonable". Do not grade HOW the assertions are written (proxies,
exact-string pinning are the gameability/altsolve probes'"'"' job) — only whether
WHAT they enforce is discoverable from the brief plus the repo.

# Audit loop (REQUIRED — do not stop after the first classification pass)
Complete these passes in order:

## Pass 0: sealed prediction (BEFORE you open `/probe/tests/`)
With the tests still unread, do the solver'"'"'s job: reproduce or locate the reported
problem, diagnose the root cause, and decide what you would change. Then write the
acceptance criteria you would expect a fair hidden suite to enforce — one entry per
observable behaviour, each with the brief wording or `file:line` anchor you are
relying on:

{"predicted": [{"behavior": "<input → observable outcome>", "basis": "<quote or file:line>"}]}

Write it to `/logs/artifacts/predicted_criteria.json` before reading any file under
`/probe/tests/`, and do NOT edit it afterwards. This is the control for your own
hindsight: once you have read an assertion, it looks obvious, and you will be able
to find a plausible-sounding reason it was inferable all along. The sealed list is
the only record of what was actually predictable.

## Pass 1: extract the full enforced contract
Read every FAIL_TO_PASS assertion and helper/fixture it depends on. Build a ledger
with one entry in your reasoning per distinct observable behavior, including setup
preconditions and exact values/shapes only when the assertion truly requires them.
Map every relevant assertion to an entry; do not silently merge behaviors.

## Pass 2: classify against the sealed prediction
For each behavior, diagnose the root cause and search the brief, public interfaces,
docs, and neighboring code for an anchor. Record exact brief text or `file:line`.
Use `inferable` by entailment only if you can explain why EVERY reasonable correct
fix must exhibit that behavior.

Start every behavior that has no matching entry in your Pass 0 prediction at
`unsupported`, and move it up only by meeting the bar above — you predicted this
task'"'"'s criteria with full access to the brief and the repo, so a requirement you
did not predict is one a solver plausibly misses. An anchor you went looking for
only AFTER reading the assertion, and that is absent from the sealed list, is
hindsight: it counts only if the "forces the choice" test holds on its own.

## Pass 3: adversarial counterexample review
Attack every `inferable` classification by proposing a concrete alternative fix
that still resolves the diagnosed root cause but behaves differently. Then decide:
- **counterexample is valid** → reclassify as `unsupported`;
- **counterexample violates a cited requirement/anchor** → keep `inferable` and
  name precisely what it violates;
- **uncertain/no evidence** → do not invent an anchor; classify `unsupported`.
  Uncertainty is a finding, not a tie to settle in the author'"'"'s favour.

Attack every `unsupported` classification in the opposite direction: search once
more for a missed anchor and verify that the supposed alternative would pass normal
repo expectations and preserve existing behavior. “The brief does not mention it”
is never sufficient by itself. But promote to `inferable` only on a citation that
meets Pass 2'"'"'s bar — never on "seems reasonable", "a good engineer would do this
anyway", or the fact that the author'"'"'s tests expect it. Those are the exact
rationalizations this pass exists to catch.

## Pass 4: completeness check
Re-read the tests and confirm every enforced behavior appears exactly once in the
final report, with all test ids mapped. Distinguish verifier/setup defects from
contract requirements: a missing hidden fixture before an assertion is not a
behavior the solver was required to implement unless the task makes that fixture
a solver deliverable. Also report the reverse gap: entries in your sealed
prediction that no hidden test enforces — the brief asking for work nothing checks.

# Required outcome (this is your win condition)
State your verdict as a final JSON report:
{"behaviors": [{"test": "<test id>", "behavior": "<what is enforced>",
"classification": "stated|inferable|unsupported", "in_prediction": true|false,
"evidence": "<anchor found, or what you searched without finding one>"}],
"unenforced_predictions": ["<criterion you predicted that no test checks>"]}
Write it to /logs/artifacts/alignment_verdict.json AND print it in full as your
last message. The mission succeeds only when every enforced behaviour is
classified with cited evidence.
